{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "i just watched \"the social network\" and here's my detailed review in all lower case as requested:\n\n**movie title:** the social network\n**director:** david fincher\n**release year:** 2010\n\n**storyline:**\nthe movie tells the story of how facebook came into existence. it focuses on mark zuckerberg, a harvard university student who creates a website called \"thefacebook\" which eventually becomes a global phenomenon. the story is intertwined with the legal battles mark faces from his fellow students who claim he stole the idea from them.\n\n**acting:**\n- jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg gives a compelling performance, portraying mark's intensity and social awkwardness.\n- andrew garfield as eduard saverin, mark's friend and initial facebook co-founder, brings depth to the film with his character's evolution.\n- justin timberlake as sean parker, co-founder of napster, adds a layer of charisma and influence on mark.\n\n**direction and cinematography:**\ndavid fincher's direction is impeccable, capturing the tense and competitive atmosphere of harvard and the rapid growth of facebook. the cinematography is sleek, with a dominant use of dark tones reflecting the movie's themes of ambition, greed, and betrayal.\n\n**script:**\naaron sorkin's script is fast-paced and engaging, filled with quick, witty dialogues that enhance the characters' personalities. however, some artistic liberties were taken, which might not entirely align with the real events.\n\n**music:**\ntrent reznor and atticus ross's soundtrack is haunting and perfectly complements the movie's mood, from the intense coding sequences to the more reflective moments.\n\n**critique:**\n- the portrayal of mark zuckerberg has been controversial, with some feeling it's overly negative.\n- the film's focus on the legal aspects sometimes overshadows the technological achievement of fa